From a52c50074fbd147a79e62b4d4549b627d979c245 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:28:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): spin at ggml_barrier with OpenMP too

With GGML_OPENMP, ggml_barrier was an omp barrier, which waits as
OMP_WAIT_POLICY says. koishi sets PASSIVE (and GOMP_SPINCOUNT=0) so that
idle workers sleep between graphs. Then every barrier inside a graph
puts the threads to sleep in the kernel and wakes them again, and a
decode or MTP verify graph has a barrier after nearly every node.

ggml_barrier now uses the atomic spin barrier of the non-OpenMP build in
both builds. Inside a graph every thread is busy until the graph ends,
so a wait is short. Between graphs the workers still wait as
OMP_WAIT_POLICY says: the omp parallel region and its end barrier are
unchanged.

GGML_CPU_BARSTAT on a CPU-only 4-token verify step (koishi, node 0, 18
threads, one run each): 34.1% of the thread time was barrier wait, and
GOMP_SPINCOUNT=200000 cut it to 13.9% and the step from 325.6 to
280.5 ms.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01C6MCJC4HTKxvuGjKjqDuqQ
---
 ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c | 8 ++++----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
index bdbb94e90..db75962b6 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -580,9 +580,10 @@ void ggml_barrier(struct ggml_threadpool * tp) {
         return;
     }
 
-#ifdef GGML_USE_OPENMP
-    #pragma omp barrier
-#else
+    // A spin barrier with OpenMP too. Every thread of a graph is busy until its end, so none
+    // waits long here. An omp barrier waits as OMP_WAIT_POLICY says, and with PASSIVE (set so
+    // that idle workers sleep between graphs) each barrier puts the threads to sleep and wakes
+    // them through the kernel.
     int n_passed = atomic_load_explicit(&tp->n_barrier_passed, memory_order_relaxed);
 
     // enter barrier (full seq-cst fence)
@@ -609,7 +610,6 @@ void ggml_barrier(struct ggml_threadpool * tp) {
     #else
     atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_seq_cst);
     #endif
-#endif
 }
 
 void ggml_threadpool_chunk_set(struct ggml_threadpool * tp, int value) {
